On 4/25/2017 2:08 PM, Philip Leifeld wrote:
> Hi there,
>
> TL;DR: My custom user term generates different values each time I call
> it using 'summary'.
>
> The long version:
>
> I have a question about the ergm.userterms package. I've been trying to
> implement an absdiff term for the first mode in bipartite networks. At
> some point, I want to add alpha and beta weights as in the b1nodematch
> term (see Bomiriya et al), but for now I'm happy without these weights
> for testing purposes. I am experimenting with non-edges as well. The
> statistic is supposed to have the following form:
>
> h_\text{b1absdiff} = \sum_i \sum_j \sum_{k \neq i} N_{ij} N_{kj} \vert
> x_i - x_k \vert + (1 - N_{ij}) (1 - N_{kj}) \vert x_i - x_k \vert
>
> where N is the network matrix and x is a metric nodal covariate. That
> is, absolute differences between the covariate of nodes i and k should
> be summed over all k nodes that are also connected to node j of the
> second partition, and absolute differences should also be summed up
> where neither i nor k is connected to j, and that second sum should be
> optional. Except for the optional non-edges part, this is really similar
> to b1nodematch.
>
> Here is what I have written up:
>
> InitErgmTerm.b1absdiff <- function(nw, arglist, ...) {
>     a <- check.ErgmTerm(nw,
>                         arglist,
>                         directed = FALSE,
>                         bipartite = TRUE,
>                         varnames = c("attrname", "nonedges"),
>                         vartypes = c("character", "logical"),
>                         required = c(TRUE, FALSE),
>                         defaultvalues = list(NULL, FALSE))
>     if (is.null(a$attrname)) {
>       stop("'attrname' must be provided.")
>     } else if (length(a$attrname) != 1) {
>       stop("'attrname' must contain the name of exactly one nodal
>            attribute.")
>     } else {
>       nodecov <- get.node.attr(nw, a$attrname)
>       coef.names <- paste0("b1absdiff.", a$attrname)
>       ne <- ifelse(a$nonedges == TRUE, 1, 0)
>     }
>     list(name = "b1absdiff",
>         coef.names = coef.names,
>         pkgname = "ergm.userterms",
>         inputs = c(nodecov, ne),
>         dependence = TRUE
>     )
> }
>
> CHANGESTAT_FN(d_b1absdiff) {
>     int i;
>     Vertex h, t, k;
>     double diff1_edge, diff1_noedge, diff2_edge, diff2_noedge;
>     int nonedges = INPUT_PARAM[N_INPUT_PARAMS - 1];
>     Vertex bip = BIPARTITE;
>     double diff_current = 0.0;
>
>     ZERO_ALL_CHANGESTATS(i);
>     FOR_EACH_TOGGLE(i) {
>       t = TAIL(i);
>       h = HEAD(i);
>       diff1_edge = 0.0;
>       diff1_noedge = 0.0;
>       diff2_edge = 0.0;
>       diff2_noedge = 0.0;
>
>       for (k = 0; k < N_NODES; k++) {
>         if (IS_OUTEDGE(k, h) && t < bip && k < bip && h >= bip &&
>             t != k) {
>           diff_current = fabs(INPUT_PARAM[t - 1] - INPUT_PARAM[k - 1]);
>           if (IS_OUTEDGE(t, h)) {
>             diff1_edge = diff1_edge + diff_current;
>           } else {
>             diff1_noedge = diff1_noedge + diff_current;
>           }
>         } else if (!IS_OUTEDGE(k, h) && nonedges == 1 && t < bip &&
>                    k < bip && h >= bip && t != k) {
>           diff_current = fabs(INPUT_PARAM[t - 1] - INPUT_PARAM[k - 1]);
>           if (IS_OUTEDGE(t, h)) {
>             diff2_edge = diff2_edge + diff_current;
>           } else {
>             diff2_noedge = diff2_noedge + diff_current;
>           }
>         }
>       }
>       CHANGE_STAT[0] += (IS_OUTEDGE(t, h) ? (diff2_edge - diff1_edge) :
>                         (diff1_noedge - diff2_noedge));
>       TOGGLE_IF_MORE_TO_COME(i);
>     }
>     UNDO_PREVIOUS_TOGGLES(i);
> }
>
> The first part of this code is the ErgmInit code, and the second part is
> the change statistic in C. This code works reasonably well (I think; but
> I will need to conduct more tests). The diff_edge and diff_noedge parts
> may be a bit redundant, but I don't care for now.
>
> Now I'd like to abstract a bit more and supply a similarity or distance
> matrix as an argument. So the change statistic should not compute the
> absolute distance, but the user supplies an m x m distance matrix. That
> will be useful because I can then supply node matches, absolute
> differences, one-mode network relations and many more things:
>
> h_\text{b1homophily} = \sum_i \sum_j \sum_{k \neq i} N_{ij} N_{kj}
> X_{ik} + (1 - N_{ij}) (1 - N_{kj}) X_{ik}
>
> where X is the square covariate matrix with the same number of rows and
> columns as N has rows. Here is my attempt at writing this down as code:
>
> InitErgmTerm.b1homophily <- function(nw, arglist, ...) {
>     a <- check.ErgmTerm(nw,
>                         arglist,
>                         directed = FALSE,
>                         bipartite = TRUE,
>                         varnames = c("mat", "nonedges"),
>                         vartypes = c("matrix", "logical"),
>                         required = c(TRUE, FALSE),
>                         defaultvalues = list(NULL, FALSE))
>     if (is.null(a$mat)) {
>       stop("'mat' must be provided.")
>     } else if (class(a$mat) != "matrix") {
>       stop("'mat' must be an m x m matrix with similarity or dissimarity
>            values for the first mode.")
>     } else {
>       coef.names <- paste("b1homophily",
>                           as.character(sys.call(0)[[3]][2]), sep = ".")
>       ne <- ifelse(a$nonedges == TRUE, 1, 0)
>       m <- as.numeric(a$mat)
>     }
>     list(name = "b1homophily",
>         coef.names = coef.names,
>         pkgname = "ergm.userterms",
>         inputs = c(m, ne),
>         dependence = TRUE
>     )
> }
>
> CHANGESTAT_FN(d_b1homophily) {
>     int i;
>     Vertex h, t, k;
>     double diff1_edge, diff1_noedge, diff2_edge, diff2_noedge;
>     int nonedges = INPUT_PARAM[N_INPUT_PARAMS - 1];
>     Vertex bip = BIPARTITE;
>     double diff_current = 0.0;
>
>     ZERO_ALL_CHANGESTATS(i);
>     FOR_EACH_TOGGLE(i) {
>       t = TAIL(i);
>       h = HEAD(i);
>       diff1_edge = 0.0;
>       diff1_noedge = 0.0;
>       diff2_edge = 0.0;
>       diff2_noedge = 0.0;
>
>       for (k = 0; k < N_NODES; k++) {
>         if (IS_OUTEDGE(k, h) && t < bip && k < bip && h >= bip &&
>             t != k) {
>           diff_current = INPUT_PARAM[(k - 1) * bip + t - 1];
>           if (IS_OUTEDGE(t, h)) {
>             diff1_edge = diff1_edge + diff_current;
>           } else {
>             diff1_noedge = diff1_noedge + diff_current;
>           }
>         } else if (!IS_OUTEDGE(k, h) && nonedges == 1 && t < bip &&
>                    k < bip && h >= bip && t != k) {
>           diff_current = INPUT_PARAM[(k - 1) * bip + t - 1];
>           if (IS_OUTEDGE(t, h)) {
>             diff2_edge = diff2_edge + diff_current;
>           } else {
>             diff2_noedge = diff2_noedge + diff_current;
>           }
>         }
>       }
>       CHANGE_STAT[0] += (IS_OUTEDGE(t, h) ? (diff2_edge - diff1_edge) :
>                         (diff1_noedge - diff2_noedge));
>       TOGGLE_IF_MORE_TO_COME(i);
>     }
>     UNDO_PREVIOUS_TOGGLES(i);
> }
>
> As you can see, this code is nearly identical to the previous code
> chunk. The only difference is that a matrix is supplied by the user, and
> the C code computes the index of the respective similarity or
> dissimilarity value in the INPUT_PARAM vector before using it (instead
> of computing the absolute difference).
>
> However, the statistic seems to return different values each time it is
> used. I don't understand why this is the case since I don't modify any
> values in the input parameter vector. Any hint would be greatly appreciated.
>
> Here is a simulation that illustrates the problem:
>
> library("statnet")
> library("ergm.userterms")
> set.seed(12345)
>
> mat <- matrix(rbinom(200, 1, 0.15), nrow = 20)
> node_cov <- rnorm(30, 0, 1)
> similarity <- matrix(rnorm(200, 0, 1), nrow = 20)
> nw <- network(mat, bipartite = TRUE, directed = FALSE)
> set.vertex.attribute(nw, "nodecov", node_cov)
>
> model <- ergm(nw ~ edges + b1absdiff("nodecov"))
>
> sapply(1:100, function(x) summary(nw ~ b1absdiff("nodecov")))
> sapply(1:100, function(x) summary(nw ~ b1homophily(similarity)))
>
> Here, the second-to-last line of code returns 100 times the same value
> while the last line of code generates slightly different values each
> time it is called on the same network. Why does this happen? Is this
> because of some previous toggles I need to reset? Or some trivial coding
> mistake on my part? I'm having a hard time wrapping my head around this
> right now.
>
> Many thanks,
>
> Philip
